<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alster</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alster is a river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> that rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> and flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. In the city it was dammed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to drive mills, forming the two lakes of the Inner and Outer Alster that are central to Hamburg's townscape. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammaburg">Hammaburg</a> fort, from which the city grew, stood near its confluence with the Elbe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alster.jpg" alt="Alster" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_Binnenalster_and_Au%C3%9Fenalster_(view_from_the_southwest).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
